atorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-65582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{20}^{∘}"] ["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "{80}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{45}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "{30}^{∘}"] ["math" "{60}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =6+2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =8 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})"] ["math" "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})"] ["math" "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.896\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={5}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =25+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$ \n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.689)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{40}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.968)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.103)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-11291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)≈$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.955\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =8+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =24 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.156)"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.916)"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-1.054)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.854)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-80147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-0.955)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & =1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4\\cdot 5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)"] ["math" "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.150)"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+0.464)"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.351)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-107577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.675\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =16+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.675)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{41}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-327980" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.675"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-274609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-274609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-274609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-274609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-274609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{35}^{∘})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{15}^{∘})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-30578" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cab1d110fad91a5454cc07913f68c920bed3a02a2543671beb0be3c79578ed7e"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{13}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "q-65574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{13}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/state.json" "file_sha256" "b268fa94565c1db9deea58ee11f8fdf2750b0acf594557f8dfa6a48f0b6a47b2"} {"question" "e-3542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3542.json" "file_sha256" "c1fb4e3d443249360f5a52096be41d4f320142ccf329458f4866103e8664a2d2"} {"question" "e-3542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x   = R\\sin(x+\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x    = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\sqrt{2}}^{2}+{\\sqrt{7}}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2+7 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3542.json" "file_sha256" "c1fb4e3d443249360f5a52096be41d4f320142ccf329458f4866103e8664a2d2"} {"question" "e-3541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ  <       \\phi <        90^\\circ$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $\\phi?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3541.json" "file_sha256" "0a96205f678aa77e372b64aa2d795c8ef0f0ff00f9ee98c1f119f31eb4ceea6d"} {"question" "e-3541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi ) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3541.json" "file_sha256" "0a96205f678aa77e372b64aa2d795c8ef0f0ff00f9ee98c1f119f31eb4ceea6d"} {"question" "e-3543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3543.json" "file_sha256" "17735fd40e97edad3021a34b6a3b25f15458e4d757767e71f2edca689cdf9573"} {"question" "e-3543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x    = R\\sin(x-\\phi) ,\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x   = (R \\cos \\phi ) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi )\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.588\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3543.json" "file_sha256" "17735fd40e97edad3021a34b6a3b25f15458e4d757767e71f2edca689cdf9573"} {"question" "e-3544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left( 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3544.json" "file_sha256" "32494c2a702357606360d7219ccf8598c662b12452dbbaa2bc9f51c1ab25aad2"} {"question" "e-3544" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x   = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -( R \\sin \\phi )\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038530/example-3544.json" "file_sha256" "32494c2a702357606360d7219ccf8598c662b12452dbbaa2bc9f51c1ab25aad2"}] "usage" {"q-30588" [] "e-3543" [] "q-327669" [] "q-65574" [] "q-11296" [] "q-274609" [] "q-80147" [] "q-30580" [] "q-80135" [] "e-3544" [] "q-327979" [] "q-107565" [] "e-3541" [] "e-3542" [] "q-107577" []} "basis" 1312 "retractions" [[17592186096883 :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\sin x + 3\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin (x + ϕ)$ with $R > 0$, what is the value of $R$?"] [17592186097135 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n$\\sin x + \\sqrt{3}\\cos x =$"] [17592186096330 :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R$?"] [17592186096330 :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x + ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ + \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x + R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = R\\sin(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = \\sqrt{2}; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= \\sqrt{2}^{2} + \\sqrt{7}^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 2 + 7 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nR &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$."] [17592186096329 :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ < \\phi < 90^\\circ$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $\\phi$?"] [17592186096329 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 2; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= 1 \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096331 :question/problem "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186096331 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi)\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.588rad\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= (3)^{2} + (2)^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 4 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 13 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$"] [17592186096332 :question/problem "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$."] [17592186096332 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\cos (x + ϕ) &= R(\\cos x\\cos ϕ - \\sin x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\cos x - R\\sin ϕ\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -(R \\sin \\phi)\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\sin ϕ)^{2} + (R\\cos ϕ)^{2} &= 3^{2} + (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\sin^{2} ϕ + \\cos^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 3 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 12 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186093706 17592186093707 17592186093708 17592186093709 17592186093710 17592186095173 17592186095174 17592186095175 17592186095176 17592186095177 17592186266559 17592186266560 17592186266561 17592186266562 17592186266563 17592186266573 17592186266574 17592186266575 17592186266576 17592186266577 17592186266597 17592186266604 17592186266605 17592186266606 17592186266607 17592186266608 17592186266611 17592186266612 17592186266622 1759218626